    "q-95351": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95351",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Which of the following are orthogonal projection matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} & -\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if ${Q}^{2}=Q$ and ${Q}^{T}=Q.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$ is *not* an orthogonal projection matrix. We have\n${A}^{T}=A$\nbut \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is an orthogonal projection matrix. Indeed,\n${B}^{T}=B$\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{2} & =B\\cdot B \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} & -\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} & -\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{100}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{100}[\\begin{aligned}90 & -30 \\\\ -30 & 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} & -\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is *not* an orthogonal projection matrix. We have\n${C}^{T}=C$\nbut \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{2} & =C\\cdot C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
