    "q-95366": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95366",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}{p}_{11} & {p}_{12} & {p}_{13} \\\\ {p}_{21} & {p}_{22} & {p}_{23} \\\\ {p}_{31} & {p}_{32} & {p}_{33}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that the vector $a$ above spans ${S}^{⊥},$ and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of ${p}_{23}+{p}_{31}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the orthogonal projection matrix onto ${S}^{⊥}=\\text{Span}\\{a\\}.$\n\nUsing the formula for the orthogonal projection matrix\n\n$$\n{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=a({a}^{T}\\,a)^{-1}{a}^{T},\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={18}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that ${P}_{S}+{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =I-{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}17 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 17 & -4 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${p}_{23}+{p}_{31}=(-4)+4=0.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
